**§0 Utils**

```js
const V = 1; // voxel size base
function mulberry32(a){return function(){a|=0;a=a+0x6D2B79F5|0;let t=Math.imul(a^a>>>15,1|a);t=t+Math.imul(t^t>>>7,61|t)^t;return ((t^t>>>14)>>>0)/4294967296;}}
const rnd = mulberry32(1337);
function rr(a,b){return a+rnd()*(b-a);}
function ri(a,b){return Math.floor(rr(a,b+1));}
function pick(arr){return arr[Math.floor(rnd()*arr.length)];}
```

Value noise 2D:
```js
function makeNoise2D(seed){
  const r = mulberry32(seed);
  const p = new Uint8Array(512);
  const perm = [];
  for(let i=0;i<256;i++) perm[i]=i;
  for(let i=255;i>0;i--){const j=Math.floor(r()*(i+1));const t=perm[i];perm[i]=perm[j];perm[j]=t;}
  for(let i=0;i<512;i++)p[i]=perm[i&255];
  const fade=t=>t*t*t*(t*(t*6-15)+10);
  const grad=(h,x,y)=>{const u=(h&1)?x:-x,v=(h&2)?y:-y;return u+v;};
  return (x,y)=>{
    const X=Math.floor(x)&255, Y=Math.floor(y)&255;
    x-=Math.floor(x); y-=Math.floor(y);
    const u=fade(x),v=fade(y);
    const A=p[X]+Y,B=p[X+1]+Y;
    const n00=grad(p[A],x,y), n10=grad(p[B],x-1,y);
    const n01=grad(p[A+1],x,y-1), n11=grad(p[B+1],x-1,y-1);
    const nx0=n00+u*(n10-n00), nx1=n01+u*(n11-n01);
    return nx0+v*(nx1-nx0); // roughly [-1,1]
  };
}
```

**§1 Voxel batch builder**

```js
class VoxelBatch {
  constructor(){ this.items=[]; }
  add(x,y,z,color, sx=1,sy=1,sz=1){ this.items.push(x,y,z,sx,sy,sz,color); }
  build(mat){ 
    const n=this.items.length/7;
    const g=new THREE.InstancedMesh(unitBox, mat, n);
    const m=new THREE.Matrix4(); const c=new THREE.Color();
    for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
      const o=i*7;
      m.makeScale(this.items[o+3],this.items[o+4],this.items[o+5]);
      m.setPosition(this.items[o],this.items[o+1],this.items[o+2]);
      g.setMatrixAt(i,m);
      g.setColorAt(i,c.set(this.items[o+6]));
    }
    g.instanceMatrix.needsUpdate=true;
    if(g.instanceColor) g.instanceColor.needsUpdate=true;
    g.computeBoundingSphere(); // maybe
    return g;
  }
}
```
Note: setColorAt creates instanceColor on first call. In r160, `setColorAt` requires instanceColor initialized — `setColorAt` does: `if (this.instanceColor === null) this.instanceColor = new InstancedBufferAttribute(new Float32Array(this.count*3), 3)`. Yes r160 handles it.

Note on colors: `new THREE.Color(hex)` with ColorManagement enabled treats hex as sRGB and converts to linear working space. Good. But passing a Color instance to setColorAt — `setColorAt(index, color)` does `color.toArray(this.instanceColor.array, index*3)`. Good.

Important: I must be careful that `setColorAt` uses `color.toArray` which writes the *working color space* (linear-srgb) values. Yes since the Color was already converted at construction. Good.

Materials: MeshStandardMaterial? For a stylized voxel garden, `MeshLambertMaterial` is cheaper and has no roughness/metalness concerns. But Standard looks better with tone mapping and env. With no env map, Standard looks flat-ish. Lambert responds to directional + hemisphere nicely and is much cheaper. Given many instances + shadows, I'll use **MeshLambertMaterial** for most, which gives clean flat voxel look, and it supports emissive? Lambert has `emissive`. Yes MeshLambertMaterial supports emissive + emissiveMap. Good for lanterns.

Hmm, but Lambert doesn't do specular highlights. For a voxel look that's fine and arguably better (flat shading). Let's use Lambert.

Actually, for nicer look I might use MeshStandardMaterial with flatShading... Lambert is fine, flat shading inherent. Go Lambert.

Shadows: instanced meshes castShadow/receiveShadow.

