Let me use voxel-aligned Y: y index k, world y = k*VS + VS/2. So the voxel occupies [k*VS, (k+1)*VS].

Let me define `VOX_Y(k) = (k+0.5)*VS`.

Ground top voxel index for a column: `kt = floor(top/VS)` where top is the desired surface height. Fill k from `kb` to `kt`.

Set: general ground top ≈ 1.0-1.6 world → kt = 2 or 3 (since 1.0/0.5=2, 1.6/0.5=3.2). So ground surface is 2-3 voxels = 1.0-2.0 world. Hmm that's tall-ish. Let me target ground surface ~1.0-1.5 world. kt = 2 mostly.

kb = -3 (so 5-6 voxels per column). 64*64*6 = 24576. Too many. Let me reduce: the bottom 3 layers are hidden. I'll only fill from kt-2 to kt (3 voxels: surface + 2 hidden) and add a perimeter skirt going down to -4 at the boundary so no floating edges. 4096 × 3 = 12288. Good.

Actually the hidden interior layers are invisible but still cost tris. Alternative: fill only the top voxel (kt) and a second (kt-1), plus a full skirt on the 4 border columns. With a low camera you might see under the terrain at the border... The border skirt handles that. Let me do 3 deep uniformly for simplicity and safety — 12k voxels, fine. Actually let me do 2 deep + skirt to 5 deep at borders. Saves a bit. Eh, 3 deep is simpler; let's just do 3 deep + deeper border skirt. 12-14k terrain voxels.

Hmm — one important detail: shadows. Terrain receiving shadows is good. Terrain casting shadows on itself creates nice relief. With a flat-ish terrain, self-shadowing is subtle. Let me have terrain castShadow = false (saves a shadow-pass over 13k instances) and receiveShadow = true. But then the pond basin won't cast shadow... that's fine, it's concave. Actually the terrain silhouette shadow on itself would be nice at sunrise/sunset. But perf: shadow pass would render all instances. 13k boxes = 156k tris. It's fine honestly. But let me keep terrain castShadow=false for perf and instead rely on trees/pagoda shadows. Hmm, without terrain self-shadowing the relief looks flat.

Let me do: terrain castShadow = true. Total instances ~28k → shadow pass ~340k tris. Modern GPU: trivial. Weak GPU (swiftshader in my headless test): slower but it's just for verification. I'll add adaptive quality.

Decision: terrain castShadow true.

**§4 Extras**: rocks (irregular blobs of grey voxels), stone lanterns, bamboo (thin green columns with node segments), fence (dark wood posts + rails around perimeter), stepping stones.

**§5 Pagoda** at (-7.5, -6.5):

Design (in voxel units, u = voxel, VS=0.5):
- Stone base/foundation: 3 tiers of stone platform, footprint 20u → 18u → 16u wide, each 1u tall, stone grey.
- Ground level around y index ~2-3. Let me build pagoda in local voxel coords and place with an offset.

Let me define pagoda local space: center at (0,0), y from 0 upward in voxel units.

Tiers (5):
tier i (0..4):
- floor slab: width W_i, 1u thick
- pillars at corners: 4 pillars 1x1, height H
- walls: panels between pillars, 1u thick, inset 1u
- railing: 1u tall ring outside the walls
- roof: big overhanging, width W_i + 8, with stepped/upswept profile

Widths: W = [14, 12, 10, 8, 6]
Heights: [7, 6.5, 6, 5.5, 5]
Roof overhang: +8, +7, +6, +5, +4 → roof widths [22, 19, 16, 13, 10]

Roof construction (the iconic upswept look): For a roof of half-width R, build layers from bottom (widest) to top:
- Actually the shape: the eave is the widest and lowest layer; as you go up, it narrows. The corners flare upward.
- Build: for layer j from 0 (bottom) to nLayers-1, half-width r_j = R - j, and y offset = j * 1 (each layer up). Then for corner voxels, add extra upward: voxels whose |x|>r-3 and |z|>r-3 get an extra +2 or +3 in y, increasing toward the corner → the upswept corner.
- Also make the roof thickness 1u and add a second layer beneath the eave for a thicker eave edge.

Let me write a `roof(cx, cz, baseY, halfW, layers)`:
```js
function addRoof(cx,cy,cz,halfW,layers,colTile,colEdge){
  for(let j=0;j<layers;j++){
    const r = halfW - j;
    const y = cy + j;
    for(let x=-r;x<=r;x++) for(let z=-r;z<=r;z++){
      // ring only: hollow interior for performance
      if (Math.abs(x)<r-1 && Math.abs(z)<r-1) continue;
      let yy = y;
      // corner upsweep
      const cx2=Math.abs(x), cz2=Math.abs(z);
      if(cx2>=r-3 && cz2>=r-3){
        const t = Math.max(cx2,cz2) - (r-3); // 0..3
        yy += Math.round(t*t*0.9); // flare
      }
      // eave edge slightly darker
      batch.add(voxX(cx+x), y+yy, voxZ(cz+z), (j===0)?colEdge:colTile);
    }
  }
}
```
Hmm, if the corner is lifted, there's a gap under it — that's fine and actually correct (the roof corner sweeps up leaving space). But visually floating corners can look broken. Let me add a small fill under the lifted corners at the eave layer. Actually the layer below... Since it's the top layer of that roof and we go up narrowing, lifting corner voxels leaves a hole. Let me instead not lift them away but add them: build the corner columns incrementally — i.e., at corner positions, add voxels from y up to y+flare. That fills the corner solidly which looks like a thick upswept corner. Simpler and looks good.

```js
// corner column fill
for(let x=-r;x<=r;x++) for(let z=-r;z<=r;z++){
  if (inInterior) continue;
  let lift=0;
  const ax=Math.abs(x), az=Math.abs(z);
  if(ax>=r-3&&az>=r-3){ const t=Math.max(ax,az)-(r-3); lift=Math.round(t*t*0.85); }
  for(let f=0;f<=lift;f++){ add(x, y+f, z, color); }
}
```
That fills corner columns from base up. 

Also the very top of the roof should have a flat cap. With layers narrowing to r=halfW-layers+1, if halfW=11, layers=7 → r goes 11..5. So the top ring is 11x11 outer, 5x5 inner hole at the top. Hmm, that leaves a hole. I need a cap. Let me make the roof a solid pyramid-ish: at each layer j, fill the full square if r <= j? Or just fill the full square at the topmost layer.

Simpler: make roofs **solid** at the top: fill ring for j < layers-1, and full square for j = layers-1. With halfW=11, layers=8: j=0..7, r=11..4. At j=7, r=4 → fill full 9x9. That's a decent cap. But then the roof is a stepped pyramid with flat top — that's fine and reads as a roof (the spire sits on it).

Actually traditional pagoda roofs are hipped with a flat-ish top platform. So solid top is correct. Good.

Let me reduce cost: roofs are rings mostly. Roof1: halfW=11, layers=8: sum of ring sizes: ring at r=11 is 23x23-21x21 = 529-441=88. r=10: 441-361=80. r=9: 81-64... wait 2r+1: r=9 → 19x19 - 17x17 = 361-289=72. r=8: 17x17-15x15=289-225=64. r=7: 15²-13²=225-169=56. r=6: 13²-11²=169-121=48. r=5: 11²-9²=121-81=40. r=4 full: 81. Total ≈ 88+80+72+64+56+48+40+81 = 529. Nice, ~530 per roof, ×5 roofs ≈ 2000 (smaller ones much less). Plus corner lift fills. Great.

Hmm wait, the corner lift makes those columns f+1 tall which adds ~ (rings in corner region) × 3 extra. Fine.

Then spire (sōrin) on top of the top roof: 
- a 3x3 pedestal, 3 tall
- 3 rings (kurin) each 5x5 ring with hole 3x3, at increasing y, dark/gold
- top jewel (hōju): 3x3 then 1x1 then 1x1 gold
Colors: dark bronze/gold #b5893c, and dark #3a2f28.

Total pagoda ~ 2000 (roofs) + tiers (5 tiers × ~ (14² floor =196 + 4 pillars×7 + walls ~ 100 + railing ~ 80) ≈ 400) = 2000 + spire 150 ≈ 4200 voxels. OK.

Let me also add hanging lanterns (chōchin) at each tier corner — small 1x2 orange voxels that glow (put them in a separate "emissive" batch so they can have emissive material). Yes: a separate batch `glowBatch` with MeshLambertMaterial({color: 0xffb347, emissive: 0xff7a1a, emissiveIntensity: varies}). And small PointLights at 2-3 of them.

**§6 Sakura trees**

```js
function buildTree(batchWood, batchBloom, cx, cz, groundY, seedR, scale)
```
Branch generation:
```js
function branch(x,y,z,dx,dy,dz,len,radius,depth){
  const steps = Math.max(2, Math.round(len));
  let px=x, py=y, pz=z;
  for(let i=0;i<steps;i++){
    // voxel at current
    addWood(px,py,pz, shade);
    // curve
    dx += noise; ...
    px+=dx; py+=dy; pz+=dz;
  }
  if(depth>0){
    const n = rnd()<0.85?2:3;
    for(let c=0;c<n;c++){ branch(px,py,pz, spread..., len*rr(0.55,0.75), depth-1) }
  } else {
    // blossom cluster at tip
    bloomCluster(px,py,pz, r)
  }
}
```

Radius: if radius>1, also add a shell around the voxel to make trunk thicker. Let me implement `addWood(px,py,pz,rad)` which adds a single voxel if rad<=1, else a 3x3-ish cross.

Blossom cluster: `for` over a sphere of radius r: if dist<r add voxel with pink color chosen by rng and by height (lighter on top). r ~ 2.5-4.0 voxels. Add ~30-60 voxels per cluster, and ~10-20 clusters per tree → 400-900 per tree × 6 trees = ~4000. 

Plus some blossom at branch mid-points for fullness.

Bloom colors: #f7c9d8 (light), #f2a8c0 (mid), #e889ab (deep), and a few near-white #fdeef3.

Trunk color: #5b4034, #4a3428, #6b4a3a.

**§7 Water shader**

```glsl
// vertex
varying vec3 vWorld; varying vec2 vUv;
uniform float uTime;
// gerstner-lite: displace y
void main(){
  vec3 p = position;
  // plane is already rotated? No - I'll build PlaneGeometry and rotate the mesh, so local p is XY.
}
```
Simpler: make a PlaneGeometry(size,size, 64,64) rotated -90° about X via `mesh.rotation.x`. Then in the shader `position` is local (x, y, 0) where local y maps to world -z... Let me instead build the plane already in XZ by creating PlaneGeometry and applying `geometry.rotateX(-Math.PI/2)`. Then position is (x, 0, z) in local space and normal (0,1,0). 

Vertex shader:
```glsl
uniform float uTime;
varying vec3 vWPos; varying vec3 vWNrm;
float wave(vec2 p, vec2 dir, float freq, float speed){
  return sin(dot(p,dir)*freq + uTime*speed);
}
void main(){
  vec3 pos = position;
  float h = 0.0;
  h += wave(pos.xz, normalize(vec2(1.0,0.3)), 1.7, 1.1)*0.035;
  h += wave(pos.xz, normalize(vec2(-0.4,1.0)), 2.3, 0.8)*0.028;
  h += wave(pos.xz, normalize(vec2(0.7,-0.8)), 4.1, 1.7)*0.016;
  pos.y += h;
  vec4 wp = modelMatrix * vec4(pos,1.0);
  vWPos = wp.xyz;
  // analytic normal
  ...
  vWNrm = normalize(mat3(modelMatrix) * nrm);
  gl_Position = projectionMatrix * viewMatrix * wp;
}
```
Computing exact analytic normals is a pain; I'll compute them by finite differences of the same wave function in the vertex shader (sample h at p, p+dx, p+dz). That's 3 evaluations — fine.

Fragment:
```glsl
uniform vec3 uDeep, uShallow, uSunColor, uSunDir, uCamPos;
uniform float uTime, uOpacity;
varying vec3 vWPos, vWNrm;
void main(){
  vec3 V = normalize(uCamPos - vWPos);
  vec3 N = normalize(vWNrm);
  float fres = pow(1.0 - max(dot(N,V),0.0), 3.0);
  vec3 base = mix(uDeep, uShallow, 0.35 + 0.25*sin(vWPos.x*0.4+uTime*0.3));
  vec3 col = mix(base, uSkyTint, fres*0.75);
  // sun specular
  vec3 H = normalize(uSunDir + V);
  float spec = pow(max(dot(N,H),0.0), 120.0) * 1.4;
